{"name":"Daddy-O","hex":"#b0af8a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"daddy-o","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Term of address for a man."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/daddy-o"}],"entries":[{"title":"Daddy-O (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Daddy-O is a 1959 B movie starring Dick Contino, Sandra Giles, and Bruno VeSota. It was directed by Lou Place and written by David Moessinger. The film is notable for its soundtrack as being the debut film score for John Williams. The film was released by American International Pictures as a double feature with Roadracers. It was later featured on the television series Mystery Science Theater 3000. Daddy-O was produced by Elmer C. Rhoden Jr., who also produced The Delinquents (1957) and The Cool and the Crazy (1958)."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/02/Daddy-O_%281958_movie_poster%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daddy-O_(film)","wikidata":"Q2251313"},{"title":"Daddy-O Daylie","blocks":[{"text":"Holmes Daylie was a radio jock on radio stations in the 1940s and 1950s who rhymed, rapped, and played bebop and was one of the early pioneers of black-appeal radio. His upbeat patter and rhyming delivery from the 1940s to 1970s on stations WAAF, WMAQ, WAIT, WGN and other broadcast outlets and television stations brought Daddy-O-Daylie, as he was known, fame and following amongst both black and white audiences. He was inducted into the Black Radio Hall of Fame in Atlanta in 1990."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daddy-O_Daylie","wikidata":"Q55417905"},{"title":"Daddy-O (rapper)","blocks":[{"text":"Glenn Kareem Bolton widely known as Daddy-O, is an American rapper and record producer. He began his career as a founding member of hip-hop group Stetsasonic, and is known for his pioneering use of sampling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Daddy-O_of_Stetsasonic.jpg/330px-Daddy-O_of_Stetsasonic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daddy-O_(rapper)","wikidata":"Q5207981"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/02/Daddy-O_%281958_movie_poster%29.jpg/250px-Daddy-O_%281958_movie_poster%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/02/Daddy-O_%281958_movie_poster%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/02/Daddy-O_%281958_movie_poster%29.jpg/250px-Daddy-O_%281958_movie_poster%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaddy-O.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Roadracers_-_Daddy-O_-_Theatre_Ad_-_May_15%2C_1959_-_Washington%2C_D.C.png/500px-Roadracers_-_Daddy-O_-_Theatre_Ad_-_May_15%2C_1959_-_Washington%2C_D.C.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Roadracers_-_Daddy-O_-_Theatre_Ad_-_May_15%2C_1959_-_Washington%2C_D.C.png/500px-Roadracers_-_Daddy-O_-_Theatre_Ad_-_May_15%2C_1959_-_Washington%2C_D.C.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Advertisement from 1959 for Daddy-O and co-feature Roadracers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoadracers_-_Daddy-O_-_Theatre_Ad_-_May_15%2C_1959_-_Washington%2C_D.C.png"}],"sounds":{"daddy-o":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-daddy-o.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e4/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-daddy-o.wav/LL-Q1